@* Deliberately narrow: register only the cache-busting tag helpers, NOT `*`.

   Registering all of Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.TagHelpers here would switch on FormTagHelper across every
   `<form method="post">` in Views/**. Each of those carries an explicit @Html.AntiForgeryToken(), so any form
   without a literal `action` would then emit a second token - and two tokens fail antiforgery validation, so
   the form would start returning 400. FormTagHelper would also start generating those forms' `action`
   attributes, which is unreliable for the custom absolute @page routes these partials are nested in.

   ScriptTagHelper and LinkTagHelper bind only to elements carrying their own asp-* attributes, so they
   cannot affect anything else. *@
@addTagHelper Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.TagHelpers.ScriptTagHelper, Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.TagHelpers
@addTagHelper Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.TagHelpers.LinkTagHelper, Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.TagHelpers
